1.) cd licensing-service
    mvn clean package dockerfile:build

2.) docker-compose up
    docker ps

3.) GET http://localhost:8082/v1/organization/optimaGrowth/license/0235431845

4.) 4. Key learning points

        Containerization: Packages the application without changing its Java code.
        Dockerfile: Defines how the application is built and run inside a container.
        Docker Compose: Defines the environment in which services run.
        Maven plugin: Integrates Docker image creation into the build process.
        Multi-stage builds: Separate build-time operations from the runtime image.
        Environment variables: Allow configuration to be supplied externally.
        Twelve-factor disposability: Services should be capable of starting and stopping quickly.
        Testing checklist
        Build the Docker image successfully.
        Confirm the image appears in docker images.
        Start the application using Docker Compose.
        Verify the container and port mapping with docker ps.
        Send the GET request and compare its response with Chapter 3.
        Stop the container and verify that its port is no longer available.

        Main takeaway: Containerization packages the licensing service  
        into a portable Docker image, while Docker Compose defines its runtime environment. 
        These concepts form the foundation for running interconnected microservices  
        in subsequent chapters.